        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Karl Marx</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1818 CE – 1883 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">5 May 1818 at Brückengasse 664, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>; third of nine children of Heinrich Marx, a lawyer, and Henriette Pressburg, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Jew from a prosperous business family; the family had converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> before his birth</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">14 March 1883 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, of bronchitis and pleurisy, aged 64 and stateless; buried on 17 March 1883 in Highgate Cemetery</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Doctor of philosophy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> (1841); editor of the Rheinische Zeitung (1842–1843) and the Neue Rheinische Zeitung (1848–1849); member of the Communist League; European correspondent of the New-York Daily Tribune (1852–1862); member of the General Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">International Workingmen's Association</a> (from 1864)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> (1848, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a> (volume I, 1867); historical materialism; the analysis of class struggle, alienation and surplus value; the Critique of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> Programme (1875); his part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a>; Marxism</span></div>
