    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> to a lawyer's family that had converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, Marx studied law and philosophy, took a doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> in 1841 and became a radical journalist, expelled in turn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>.</li><li>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> he wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> in 1848, then spent the rest of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he worked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a>, whose first volume appeared in 1867, and helped lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a>.</li><li>His followers debated how to interpret him, revolutions in many countries called themselves Marxist, and his legacy is still contested: opponents blame him for deaths in those states, while others separate his ideas from what was later done in his name.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When Marx died in 1883 he left a personal estate valued for probate at £250, and only between nine and eleven mourners, by Francis Wheen's count, attended his funeral at Highgate Cemetery.</p></aside>
    </section>

