    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1818</span><span class="rf-text">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1841</span><span class="rf-text">Awarded his doctorate by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> for a thesis on Democritean and Epicurean philosophy of nature</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1843</span><span class="rf-text">Marries Jenny von Westphalen and moves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to co-edit the Deutsch-Französische Jahrbücher</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> is first published on 21 February; Marx then returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and founds the Neue Rheinische Zeitung</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text">Ordered to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he remains for the rest of his life</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1864</span><span class="rf-text">Elected to the General Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">International Workingmen's Association</a> at its inception</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1867</span><span class="rf-text">The first volume of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a> is published</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1883</span><span class="rf-text">Dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 14 March and is buried in Highgate Cemetery</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Karl Marx was born on 5 May 1818 to Heinrich Marx and Henriette Pressburg at Brückengasse 664 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>. The family was originally non-religious Jewish but had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> before his birth; his paternal line had supplied Trier's rabbis since 1723. After Prussia's annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> in 1815 and the abrogation of Jewish emancipation, his father, a lawyer, converted to the state Evangelical Church of Prussia in order to retain his career. Heinrich Marx was a man of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and a classical liberal who took part in agitation for a constitution. Karl was the third of nine children and became the eldest son when his brother Moritz died in 1819. He and his surviving siblings were baptised into the Lutheran Church on 28 August 1824.</p><p>He was educated privately by his father until 1830, when he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> zu Trier, whose headmaster, Hugo Wyttenbach, employed many liberal humanists as teachers. In 1832 police raided the school and discovered literature promoting political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> among the students. In October 1835, at 16, Marx went to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> wishing to study philosophy and literature, but his father insisted on law as more practical. He was excused from military duty because of a "weak chest". At Bonn he joined the Poets' Club, whose political radicals were monitored by the police, and the Trier Tavern Club; in August 1836 he took part in a duel. His grades deteriorated and his father forced a transfer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Karl Marx&#8221; cites where it covers Childhood and early education: 1818–1836, Hegelianism and early journalism: 1836–1843, Family:</p><ul><li>David McLellan, Karl Marx: A Biography (Palgrave MacMillan, 2006)</li><li>Jonathan Sperber, Karl Marx: A Nineteenth-Century Life (W. W. Norton &amp; Company, 2013)</li><li>Peter Singer, Marx: A very short introduction (Oxford University Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, Marx enrolled in the law school in October 1836 but was fascinated by philosophy and became interested in Hegel, whose ideas were then widely debated. Through the Doctors Club he became involved in 1837 with the Young Hegelians, gathered around Ludwig Feuerbach and Bruno Bauer, who adopted Hegel's dialectical method to criticise established society, politics and religion from a left-wing perspective. He had become engaged to Jenny von Westphalen, a member of the petty nobility who had known him since childhood. His father died in May 1838, which diminished the family's income.</p><p>He completed his doctoral thesis, The Difference Between the Democritean and Epicurean Philosophy of Nature, in 1841. Because it was controversial among conservative professors at Berlin, he submitted it to the more liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a>, whose faculty awarded him the degree in April 1841. An academic career was barred by the government's growing opposition to classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and the Young Hegelians, so in 1842 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and became a journalist on the radical Rheinische Zeitung. Prussian censors checked every issue. After the paper published an article strongly criticising the Russian monarchy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Nicholas I requested a ban and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s government complied in 1843.</p><p>Marx married Jenny on 19 June 1843 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-kreuznach">Kreuznach</a>, seven years after their engagement, and that October the couple moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he had become co-editor of the Deutsch-Französische Jahrbücher with Arnold Ruge.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels.jpg" alt="Friedrich Engels" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Engels_portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Hall (1826–ca. 1898) (cropped and sepia tone removed), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Marx studied political economy and the French socialists and, between April and August 1844, wrote the Economic and Philosophic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a>, which treat alienated labour. On 28 August 1844 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> at the Café de la Régence, beginning a lifelong friendship. In 1845, at the request of the Prussian king, the French government shut down the paper Vorwärts! and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-guizot">François Guizot</a> expelled Marx from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, where he and Engels wrote The German Ideology, which was not published in his lifetime but only in 1932, and where he wrote The Poverty of Philosophy (1847) in reply to Proudhon.</p><p>In June 1847 the League of the Just was reorganised as the open Communist League, and Marx and Engels took part in drawing up its programme. They wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> from December 1847 to January 1848; it was first published on 21 February 1848. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> began, Marx was arrested by the Belgian authorities, who accused him of using part of an inheritance to arm Belgian workers, an allegation whose veracity is disputed. He fled to France, then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, where on 1 June he began the daily Neue Rheinische Zeitung. He was tried several times and acquitted each time, but the paper was suppressed and he was ordered to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> on 16 May 1849.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1849 he lived in extreme poverty and depended largely on Engels. From 1852 to 1862 he was European correspondent of the New-York Daily Tribune. In 1859 he published A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy, and in 1864 he was elected to the General Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a>. The first volume of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a> appeared in 1867.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Karl Marx&#8221; cites where it covers Philosophy and social thought, International relations:</p><ul><li>Craig J. Calhoun, Classical Sociological Theory (Wiley-Blackwell, 2002)</li><li>Barry Stewart Clark, Political economy: a comparative approach (ABC-CLIO, 1998)</li><li>Kevin B. Anderson, Marx at the Margins: On Nationalism, Ethnicity, and Non-Western Societies (University of Chicago Press, 2016)</li><li>Leszek Kołakowski, Main currents of Marxism: the founders, the golden age, the breakdown (W.W. Norton &amp; Company, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin.jpg" alt="Mikhail Bakunin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikhail_Bakunin_Nader_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nadar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marx's quarrels were frequent. His friendship with Arnold Ruge broke down over the Jahrbücher. In 1850 the Communist League split when August Willich and Karl Schapper urged an immediate uprising; Marx and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> called this "adventuristic", and Marx's view prevailed in the League, though he lost the same contest in the German Workers' Educational Society and resigned from it on 17 September 1850. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a> he fought the anarchists led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a>. According to Engels, the Neue Rheinische Zeitung remained "a simple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> by Marx". The biographer Werner Blumenberg wrote that his verdicts on enemies and friends alike were sometimes harsh enough to give offence.</p><p>Some of his writings have been disputed. His essay "On the Jewish Question" has been described as evidence of antisemitic views by Paul Johnson, Bernard Lewis, Hyam Maccoby and Robert S. Wistrich, a reading disputed by Wendy Brown, Robert Fine, David McLellan and Francis Wheen, among others. In the Neue Rheinische Zeitung of February 1849 he and Engels called for "the most determined use of terror" against the Slav peoples they regarded as counter-revolutionary. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, he wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was "the unconscious tool of history" in bringing a social revolution; the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy says this can sound like a rationalisation of foreign domination, but reflects his ambivalence towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>.</p><p>Critics have called his emphasis on the primacy of social relations economic determinism. Political opponents in countries associated with Marxism have blamed him for millions of deaths, while others argue for a distinction between his own influence and that of those who shaped his ideas for political purposes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-lassalle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-lassalle.jpg" alt="Ferdinand Lassalle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-lassalle">Ferdinand Lassalle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Lassalle_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philipp Graff, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the last decade of his life Marx's health declined and he became incapable of the sustained effort of earlier years, though he still commented on contemporary politics, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. His Critique of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> Programme (1875) opposed the tendency of Wilhelm Liebknecht and August Bebel to compromise with the state socialist ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-lassalle">Ferdinand Lassalle</a>, and contains the phrase "From each according to his ability, to each according to his need". In a letter to Vera Zasulich dated 8 March 1881 he considered the possibility that Russia might bypass the capitalist stage and build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> on the common ownership of land in the village mir, provided that the influences assailing it were first eliminated. Volumes II and III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a> remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> on which he worked for the rest of his life.</p><p>His wife Jenny died in December 1881, and he developed a catarrh that kept him in ill health for the last 15 months of his life. His eldest daughter died in January 1883. The catarrh brought on the bronchitis and pleurisy that killed him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 14 March 1883, when he was 64 and stateless. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a>, he was found in his armchair, "peacefully gone to sleep". He was buried on 17 March in Highgate Cemetery in an area reserved for agnostics and atheists; Engels and Liebknecht were among those who spoke. His estate was valued at £250. Engels edited and published volume II of Das Kapital in 1885 and volume III in October 1894.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mao-zedong.jpg" alt="Mao Zedong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Zedong_1950_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chen Zhengqing (1917–1966), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marx's ideas had a profound impact on world politics and thought, in particular after the 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>. His followers have debated how to interpret him, and the legacy has become contested among tendencies that each see themselves as his most accurate interpreter, among them Leninism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, Trotskyism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoism">Maoism</a> and Luxemburgism. Throughout the 20th century revolutions in dozens of countries labelled themselves Marxist, most notably the Russian Revolution, which led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Leaders such as Lenin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> have cited him as an influence.</p><p>His work contributed to the birth of modern sociology: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah-berlin">Isaiah Berlin</a> considers him the true founder of modern sociology "in so far as anyone can claim the title", and Karl Löwith considered Marx and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soren-kierkegaard">Søren Kierkegaard</a> the two greatest philosophical successors of Hegel. Historians inspired by his social theory include E. P. Thompson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-hobsbawm">Eric Hobsbawm</a>, who remarked that "one cannot say Marx died a failure"; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-gramsci">Antonio Gramsci</a> sought to understand transformative political practice in its light. Marx's family was reburied on a new site in Highgate in November 1954, with a tomb unveiled on 14 March 1956 bearing the last line of the Manifesto.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a> was named Karl-Marx-Stadt after him. In May 2018 a statue of him by the Chinese sculptor Wu Weishan, donated by the Chinese government, was unveiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. In 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added two documents in his handwriting to its Memory of the World International Register: his annotated first edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a>, volume I, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> page of the Manifesto.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Karl Marx&#8221; cites where it covers Influences, Legacy:</p><ul><li>Craig J. Calhoun, Classical Sociological Theory (Wiley-Blackwell, 2002)</li><li>Heine Andersen, Classical and modern social theory (Wiley-Blackwell, 2000)</li><li>Barry Stewart Clark, Political economy: a comparative approach (ABC-CLIO, 1998)</li><li>Paresh Chattopadhyay, Marx's Associated Mode of Production: A Critique of Marxism (Springer, 2016)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Karl Marx (1818–1883), born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, was a German philosopher and journalist who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>, wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> in 1848 and, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the first volume of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/das-kapital">Das Kapital</a> in 1867. His ideas, known as Marxism, inspired revolutions and parties in many countries, and his legacy remains contested.</em></p>

